NAME

fetch - Download HTTP or authenticated HTTPS resources without overwriting existing files

SYNOPSIS

    fetch [OPTIONS] URL...
    fetch [OPTIONS] --input-file=FILE

DESCRIPTION

Download HTTP or authenticated HTTPS resources without overwriting existing files.

OPTIONS

    -o FILE, --output=FILE — Write one URL to FILE; it must not exist.
    -q, --quiet — Suppress normal lifecycle, progress, and success output.
    -i FILE, --input-file=FILE — Read one URL per line from FILE.
    --timings — Show detailed DNS, TCP, TLS, transfer, and filesystem timings.
    -h, --help — Show this quick help.

NOTES

URLs without a scheme use http://. HTTPS uses TLS 1.3 with the system CA store, trusted UTC, and hostname verification; there is no HTTP fallback.
HTTP redirects may upgrade to HTTPS. HTTPS-to-HTTP redirects are rejected. Relative redirects, Content-Length, chunked, and connection-close responses are supported.
Multiple URLs are downloaded sequentially; automatic names gain .1, .2, and later suffixes on collision.
URL list files use one URL per line; blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored. Explicit output files are never overwritten.
Normal output reports the requested URL, one resolved IPv4 address, TCP connect, TLS authentication for HTTPS, HTTP response metadata, destination, and a completion summary.
In-place progress is used only when standard output is the interactive console and is wide enough. Redirected output receives no carriage-return progress updates.

